An argument is passed at the parameter's width, not its own
==========================================================

	f(fp, 0, 0);		/* f takes a long */

used to push two bytes for the 0.  The callee read four.  Everything
after that argument came from the wrong place, and every argument
after it was off by a slot.

Everything goes on the stack in whole slots: a long takes two of
them, anything narrower takes one.  Get the count wrong and the
callee is reading bytes the caller never wrote there.


What the callee saw
-------------------

fseek is

	fseek(FILE *fp, long off, int whence)

so it takes fp, then four bytes of off, then whence.  With the offset
pushed as a word:

	fp	correct
	off	the caller's 0 in the low half, the caller's whence
		in the high half
	whence	whatever is on the stack above the arguments

whence is therefore not 0, 1 or 2, and lseek

	default:
		errno = 22;		/* EINVAL */
		return -1;

returned without issuing the seek.  Nothing traps, so a syscall trace
shows no seek at all - which is what made this hard to see from the
outside.  fseek returned EOF, and hardly any caller in the tree
checks it.


What it cost
------------

asz assembles into a temp file, which it unlinks at once so that
closing it is enough to clean up.  At the end it seeks back to the
start of the temp and copies it into the object:

	fseek(tmpfp, 0, SEEK_SET);
	do {
		i = fread(tmpbuf, 1, sizeof(tmpbuf), tmpfp);
		...
	} while (i == sizeof(tmpbuf));

The seek did nothing, the read came back at end of file, the copy
copied nothing, and the object was written with its sixteen byte
header and no contents.  wsld then read sixteen bytes where it wanted
a module and said

	wsld: read error

That is the whole distance between this bug and the symptom: a
missing declaration, an argument two bytes short, a seek that never
happened, an empty object, and a linker complaining about a file the
assembler had just written.

The host build never saw it.  ccc.c is compiled by gcc against the
host's headers, where fseek is declared properly and gcc converts the
argument; only the Micronix build of asz, wsld, wslib and wsnm went
through pass1.


The fix
-------

pfxCall converts each argument to the type the prototype declares for
it, in argcvt (src/pass1/post.c).  Widening is a SEXT or a WIDEN
depending on the source's signedness, and a constant is relabelled
rather than wrapped, since it is emitted at the width of its type.

Narrowing was the half that was missed, and it is the same bug read
backwards: a long handed to an int parameter puts four bytes where
the callee reads two, and the callee takes the high word as the next
argument.  That direction gets a NARROW - the node a cast to the same
type would have built - and a constant is truncated where it stands,
because otherwise the wide value goes out under the narrow name.

Only long crossing the line does anything.  char and short arguments
are already pushed as words (pusharg promotes anything that is not a
long), so an int passed where the prototype says char moves the same
two bytes either way: the callee reads the low byte of the slot, and
on a little-endian machine that is the byte it wants.

Without a prototype nothing can be done and the caller has to write
the L, which is the ordinary K&R rule.

tests/run/rt_argcvt.c covers both directions, with arguments on both
sides of the offending one so that a lost slot shows up as a wrong
answer rather than as luck.  Against the compiler before the
narrowing half it fails at check 17.


What was done to the tree meanwhile
-----------------------------------

stdio.h did not declare fseek at all.  It does now, as a prototype,
which is what lets pass1 do anything here.

The call sites were also changed to pass longs themselves:

	fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_SET);
	fseek(fp, (long)(obj->file_base + seg_start), SEEK_SET);

45 of them across asz, asm, wsld, wslib, wsnm, wssize and astpp.
That was a workaround for the fseek case only, and it is now
redundant rather than wrong - the L costs nothing and says what is
meant.  Any other function taking a long is covered by the pass1
conversion wherever a prototype is in scope; the tree is written K&R,
so most calls still have none, and there the rule is unchanged.

A stale install will still show the old code.  The prototype only
helps if the header pass1 reads has it: unix/lib/include is a copy,
and until "make -C src/include target-install" runs, a reproducer
built against it sees no prototype at all and pushes the word.
